    {
      "id": "msg_0b5bf390bcc26aae006aadc56eaee487d09e27ea0fd4f8cede",
      "type": "message",
      "status": "completed",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lagerlof-gosta-berlings-saga-ea36a229384cb926\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":2,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.92,\"analysis\":\"Gösta's inward “O my people … how I love you!” clearly avows as true his newly felt love for the departing people. It undertakes no relational commitment; indeed, he does not know what he wants to do for them. The dreamlike emergence of the thought as the new emotion wells up gives moderate independent support to an expressive/reflexive reading, though it can also be understood as an intensely framed avowal.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The E force is limitedly ambiguous: the apostrophic thought is presented as an immediate outflow of newly arising feeling, but much of its effect can still be represented as T plus dreamlike, emotional delivery.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"imagined\",\"description\":\"An internally voiced thought addressed apostrophically to the departing people, not an externally spoken declaration.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"After the once-vengeful crowd has been pacified and humanized in Gösta's perception, a new collective and compassionate love arises in him for the impoverished people, including their faults and frailties. His thought is sincere within the narration and seeks eventual reciprocal love, but it neither promises action nor commits him to a defined undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“O, mitt folk … vad jag älskar dig!” is explicitly introduced by “tänkte han” (“he thought”).\",\"supports\":\"Identifies the event as inwardly imagined speech and gives it the form of a direct avowal addressed to the people.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I hans hjärta rann denna kväll upp en ny kärlek” (“That evening a new love welled up in his heart”).\",\"supports\":\"Strongly supports T and, through the presentation of words arising amid a newly emerging emotion, gives some independent support to E.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator says that he loved the whole crowd and “alla och envar, med fel och laster och skröpligheter” (“each and every one, with faults and vices and frailties”).\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the thought presents a genuine loving emotional state rather than an empty formula or unrelated use.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Han visste inte vad han ville göra för dem” (“He did not know what he wanted to do for them”).\",\"supports\":\"Argues against performative undertaking force: the love is not accompanied by a promise, obligation, or defined commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Gösta stands “som i en dröm” and later awakens from that dreamlike state; his painful, burning tenderness forces tears into his eyes.\",\"supports\":\"Supports the immediate affective framing and moderate E score, while functioning mainly as contextual evidence of intensity.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the avowal of collective love, while a moderate E score captures its presentation as a dreamlike inward effusion. P is unnecessary because no undertaking is enacted, and no residual core force requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The singular Swedish pronoun “dig” addresses the collective “mitt folk”; the love is communal and compassionate rather than romantic.\"}"
        }
      ],
      "phase": "final_answer",
      "role": "assistant"
    }
